{"text": "PLOS | songurarions\nBIOLOGY Schwalger T, Deger M, Gerstner W. PLoS Comput Biol. 2017;13(4):e1005507\n\nTowards a theory of cortical columns: From spiking neurons\nto interacting neural populations of finite size\n\nTilo Schwalger\"*, Moritz Deger!*, Wulfram Gerst\n\n1 Brain Mind Institute, School of Computer and Communication Sciences\nand School of Life Sciences, Ecole Polytechnique F\u00e9d\u00e9rale de Leusanne\n(EPFL), Lausanne, Switzerland\n2 Institute for Zoology, Faculty of Mathematics and Natural Sciences,\nUniversity of Cologne, Cologne, Germany\n\n* tilo.schwalger@epfi.ch\n\nAbstract\n\nNeural population equations such as neural mass or field models are widely used to\nstudy brain activity on a large seale, However, the relation of these models to the\nproperties of single neurons is unclear. Here we derive an equation for several\ninteracting populations at the mesoscopic seale starting from a microscopic model of\nrandomly connected generalized integrate-and-fire neuron models. Each population\nconsists of 50 ~ 2000 neurons of the same type but different populations aecount for\ndifferent neuron types. The stochastic population equations that we find reveal how\ninteract with finite-size fuctuations on the population level. Efficient integration of the\ntie mesoscopic equations reproduces the statistical behavior of the population\nies obtained from microscopic simulations of a full spiking neural network model.\ntransitions in multistable networks and synchronization in balanced networks of\nintegrate a model of a cortical microcireuit consisting of eight neuron types, which\nallows us to predict spontaneous population activities as well as evoked responses to\nthalamic input. Our theory establishes a general framework for modeling finite-size\nefficient approach to analyzing cortical circuits and computations.\n\nAuthor Summary\n\nUnderstanding the brain requires mathematical models on different spatial scales. On\n\u00abl roscopic\u201d level of nerve cells, neural spike trains ean be well predicted by\nphenomenological spiking neuron models. On a coarse scale, neural activity can be\nmodeled by phenomenological equations that summarize the total activity of m\nthousands of neurons. Such population models are widely used to model neuroimaging\ndata such as EEG, MEG or {MRI data, However, itis largely unknown how largeseale\nmodels are connected to an underlying microscale model. Linking the scales is vital for\n\u2018a correct description of rapid changes and fluctuations of the poprilation activity, and is\ncrucial for multiscale brain models. The challenge is to treat realistic spiking dynamics\n\nPLOS Computational Biology | https: //doi .org/10.1871/journal .pebi . 1005507] April 19, 2017 1 fe",
